---
import Exit from './Exit.astro';
import { placements } from '../measurement';
import { narration } from '../narrative';
import { ruleLabel } from '../catalogue';

const act = narration.actThree;

// Grouped by code, which is what the engine makes stable — the sentences are its own and are
// shown word for word. Grouping and ordering are this page's job, never the wording (§10.4).
const byCode = new Map<string, string[]>();

for (const reason of act.refusals) {
    byCode.set(reason.code, [...(byCode.get(reason.code) ?? []), reason.sentence]);
}
---

<!--
  ACT III — the theme answers for itself. §9.2, and a static block for the reason ActTwo
  gives.
-->
<section class="act" id="act-three" aria-labelledby="act-three-heading">
    <div class="shell">
        <p class="act-mark">Act three</p>

        <!--
          §9.4 — THE HINGE IS MANDATORY, and its content is constrained. The visitor has just
          been told the vocabulary is theirs; the next thing they see is a file being refused.
          Without this sentence they read a bad news where they are being offered a guarantee.
        -->
        <p class="hinge">
            Words of your own are a freedom, and this is what pays for it: slugger answers for
            the vocabulary you write, so it has to be able to refuse one.
        </p>

        <h2 id="act-three-heading">A refusal that says everything at once</h2>

        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                Here is a theme written in ten minutes and kept exactly as it was first typed.
                One run reports <strong>every</strong> reason it cannot be used — not the first,
                and not one per attempt.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card refusal">
            <p class="refused">
                Theme &ldquo;{act.theme}&rdquo; was refused for {act.refusals.length} reasons.
            </p>
            {
                [...byCode].map(([code, sentences]) => (
                    <section class="refusal-group">
                        <p class="slot-name">{code}</p>
                        <ul class="marks marks--open">
                            {sentences.map((sentence) => <li>{sentence}</li>)}
                        </ul>
                    </section>
                ))
            }
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                Every reason names its subject — the noun, the category, the key — because a
                figure on its own does not say what to fix.
            </p>
            <p>
                <strong>And a refusal only answers yes or no.</strong> Measuring answers
                <em>by how much</em>, and it works on a refused file, which is exactly when it
                is wanted:
            </p>
        </div>

        <table class="score">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Rule</th>
                    <th scope="col">Worst case</th>
                    <th scope="col">Floor</th>
                    <th scope="col">Margin</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                {
                    act.margins.map((margin) => (
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="row">{ruleLabel(margin.rule)}</th>
                            <td>{margin.worstCase.toLocaleString('en')}</td>
                            <td>{margin.limit.toLocaleString('en')}</td>
                            <td class={margin.marginValue < 0 ? 'refused' : 'passes'}>
                                {`${margin.marginValue >= 0 ? '+' : ''}${margin.marginValue.toLocaleString('en')}`}
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    ))
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="prose">
            <p>
                Knowing a noun reaches six adjectives rather than ninety-nine is what tells an
                author what to write next. A refusal would only have said it was short.
            </p>
            <p>
                <!-- §9.6 — the page never promises more than the library does. -->
                What none of this judges is taste. No rule will say an adjective is a noun badly
                used, or that a participle lends a stone an intention it cannot have. That is
                read, not computed — which is why a theme can exclude a word for one noun and
                keep it for every other.
            </p>
        </div>

        <Exit
            lead="You have seen what writing a theme costs and what answers for it. The command is what measures and registers one."
            offers={['Slugger']}
            placement={placements.afterTheRefusal}
            extra={{ href: '/themes/', label: 'Themes already written, measured by the engine' }}
        />
    </div>
</section>
